{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Premise falsified by experiment — the stated mechanism is not reachable; the real defect is different (and worse)\n\nRan the REAL `heal_multi_bundle` against the existing harness with the one fixture change this\nticket asks for: a tracker whose checked-out branch is NOT the published ref (a `sidebranch`\ncarrying its own unrelated commit). Findings below; the ticket should be rescoped or closed as\nnot-a-bug-as-described before any code is written.\n\n### 1. The bundle's HEAD and the published ref can never diverge in one non-racy heal\n\nThe produced bundle's heads:\n\n    249bc7f0… HEAD\n    249bc7f0… refs/heads/tickets\n\nSame commit, both entries. The reason is in the flow, not the fixture:\n\n- `_fold_heads` merges every fetched head INTO the worktree's HEAD and returns\n  `merged_sha = git rev-parse HEAD`, so `merged_sha` IS whatever HEAD points at.\n- `_publish_and_prune` then runs `git branch -f <ref> <merged_sha>` in exactly the branch this\n  ticket calls defective — the `current_branch != ref` case.\n\nSo the ref is force-moved ONTO the commit HEAD names. The literal `HEAD` argument therefore\nnames the same object as `refs/heads/<ref>` in every configuration: when `current_branch == ref`\nthey are the same ref, and when they differ, `branch -f` has just made them equal. The claim\nthat the bundle \"silently carries the WRONG tip alongside the correct `refs/heads/<ref>`\" does\nnot hold. A test written to AC #2 as stated would pass BEFORE any fix — there is no RED.\n\n### 2. The genuine defect: the heal folds into the worktree HEAD, publishing unrelated commits\n\nThe same probe shows what IS broken:\n\n    side reachable from clone HEAD (POLLUTION): True\n\nThe unrelated `sidebranch` commit ends up reachable from the published tickets tip. Because the\nfold merges into whatever branch is checked out, the side branch's history is merged into the\ntickets history, and `branch -f` then publishes it as the tickets ref. The wrong content reaches\nthe ref through `_fold_heads` + `branch -f`, NOT through the bundle argument — so changing\n`HEAD` to `refs/heads/<ref>` in the bundle call would bundle exactly the same polluted commit\nand satisfy none of AC #2. Meeting AC #2 requires folding against `refs/heads/<ref>` instead of\nthe worktree HEAD, which cannot be done with `git merge` on a non-checked-out branch — it needs\n`merge-tree`/`commit-tree` or a scratch worktree. That is a materially larger change than the\none-line fix this ticket specifies.\n\n### 3. Two supporting sub-claims are also not reachable\n\n- **Unborn HEAD → `fatal: ambiguous argument`** never reaches the bundle step: `git merge` on an\n  unborn HEAD fast-forwards, so `_fold_heads` succeeds and HEAD is born before publish.\n- **Detached HEAD** produces a CORRECT bundle when the detached commit is on the ref's history;\n  it is only wrong under the same pollution mechanism as §2, i.e. when the detached commit\n  carries content absent from the ref.\n\n### Suggested rescope (needs a human call)\n\n1. Rewrite this ticket to the proven defect in §2 (heal folds into the worktree HEAD and\n   publishes unrelated commits into the tickets ref), and fix `_fold_heads`; or\n2. Narrow it to cheap hardening: refuse the heal (or fail loudly) when `current_branch != ref`,\n   which provably prevents the pollution without re-engineering the merge; or\n3. Close as not-a-bug-as-described, keeping this comment as the record.\n\nNote that rebar's own tracker always has the published ref checked out, so §2 is latent for\nrebar's real stores and matters mainly for a differently-checked-out or reused tracker."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786508871634635001,"uuid":"6cbce324-1f4f-44b4-9532-4f1a5521c9b4"}